The attached image illustrates the local inflammatory response two weeks after intramuscular injection of adjuvants, comparing aluminum hydroxide adjuvant (AH) (left panel) with Nano-11 (right panel). The left panel, depicting AH, shows extensive granulomatous inflammation (asterisks) characterized by dense inflammatory cell infiltration into the skeletal muscle (M), as highlighted in the inset. In contrast, the right panel, representing Nano-11, reveals significantly less residual inflammation (smaller asterisk) with only a few macrophages in slender cords within the connective tissue surrounding the muscle (M), confirmed by the inset. This visually supports the study's finding that Nano-11 induces a transient and milder inflammatory response that resolves more quickly than the persistent granulomatous inflammation caused by aluminum adjuvants.